digraph GSN {
  rankdir=TB;
  node [fontname="Helvetica" fontsize=9];
  edge [fontname="Helvetica" fontsize=8];
  labelloc="t"; label="SafeDSE safety argument — f_both3"; fontname="Helvetica";
  C1 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C1\nIEC 61508 is the governing functional\nsafety standard; integrity is allocated as\na required Safety Integrity Level per\nactor (Q1, Q13). SIL 0 means no safety\nrequirement."];
  C2 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C2\nThe required SIL of every actor is an\ninput from a hazard and risk analysis\noutside this argument."];
  C3 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C3\nPattern catalog patterns.yaml: structure,\nplacement relations, SIL range and covered\nfault classes of each pattern, after\nArmoush, Koopman. Every pattern choice and\nplacement rule in this argument rests on\nit."];
  C4 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C4\nPlatform: 28 core slots across 10 fault\ncontainment regions, core types cortexM,\ncortexR, ppcE200. Cores actually\ninstantiated in this solution: [1, 2, 7,\n13, 14]."];
  C5 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C5\nApplications: c_rasta, modelled as\nsynchronous dataflow graphs and unfolded\nto an equivalent homogeneous graph before\nmapping."];
  C6 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C6\nFault model under consideration: random\nhardware faults and systematic software\nfaults (Q2). Patterns not covering this\nfault class were excluded from the design\nspace, and placement relations motivated\nonly by an excluded fault class were not\nposted. Systematic faults are covered only\nas far as they differ between core types\nand toolchains, or between a doer and a\nchecker with a different algorithm; design\nfaults common to diverse implementations\nneed diverse development. Not addressed:\ntransient faults, timing and omission\nfaults, faults of the interconnect, latent\nfaults, diagnostic coverage, and degraded\nmodes."];
  J1 [shape=ellipse style=filled fillcolor="#fff6d5" label="J1 (J)\nThis argument is generated from a design\nspace exploration result. It argues only\nover properties of the ARCHITECTURE and\nits DEPLOYMENT: which pattern is applied\nto which actor, where components are\nplaced relative to each other, how\nintegrity levels are provisioned per core,\nand whether the resulting schedule meets\nits timing requirement. Claims about\nimplementation correctness, detection\ncoverage and development process are\noutside what a mapping can establish and\nare carried below as undeveloped goals.\nThe SIL range of a pattern is a\nprecondition taken from the catalog,\nnecessary but not sufficient for a SIL:\nthis argument computes no failure rates,\nhardware fault tolerance, safe failure\nfraction or diagnostic coverage."];
  A1 [shape=ellipse style=filled fillcolor="#fff6d5" label="A1 (A)\nThe worst-case execution times supplied to\nthe exploration are sound upper bounds for\nevery actor on every core type it may be\nbound to. The exploration consumes these\nfigures; it does not establish them."];
  A2 [shape=ellipse style=filled fillcolor="#fff6d5" label="A2 (A)\nToken preservation (Q4): every replica\nfires on every iteration and produces on\nall of its outputs, in the fault-free and\nin the degraded case alike. Detection\nmarks a token invalid; it never withholds\none. The timing argument therefore holds\nin the degraded mode, at the cost of being\nconservative when no fault is present."];
  A3 [shape=ellipse style=filled fillcolor="#fff6d5" label="A3 (A)\nThe verifier re-checks the solution\nagainst the instance produced by the front\nend, not against the original\nspecification. The instance represents the\nplatform, execution times, pattern catalog\nand safety requirements correctly; an\nerror in the front end or in the\nspecification would be common to the\nexploration and the verifier."];
  G1 [shape=box label="G1\nThe deployed architecture of f_both3\nmeets, for every safety-related actor, the\narchitectural preconditions that the\npattern catalog states for the actor's\nrequired SIL."];
  C7 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C7\nEvidence identity (SHA-256): instance\n22352a3f0f4a3db86683fa9c2e34ff46ab82633cb2687c933fa99619bea3caf6;\nsolution\nfc270494ae525d25cbcbc73cb6e9d54af4c4b9d75b6dd8daf4249ed357f284b7;\ncheck log\nf7d997ed48478bb86115efa43137874ee69ed28ddc3beb351fc00c44a4644884.\nEvery Solution cites a record of this\ncheck log by index."];
  S1 [shape=parallelogram label="S1\nArgument over each safety-related actor in\nturn, followed by the platform-wide\nproperties on which those per-actor\narguments depend."];
  G2 [shape=box label="G2\nActor frontEnd of application c_rasta,\nrequired SIL 3: the deployment meets the\narchitectural preconditions that the\ncatalog states for SIL 3."];
  C8 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C8\nfrontEnd is unfolded into 1 concurrent\ncopy, bound to core 1, implemented at SIL\n3."];
  G3 [shape=box label="G3\nEvery copy of frontEnd and of its\nchannel_b is implemented at SIL 3 or above\nand runs on a core provisioned to at least\nthat level."];
  Sn1 [shape=circle label="Sn1\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nevery active copy of frontEnd is\nimplemented at or above its required SIL\nand runs on a core provisioned to at least\nthat level."];
  Sn2 [shape=circle label="Sn2\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nevery active copy of frontEnd's channel_b\nis implemented at or above its required\nSIL and runs on a core provisioned to at\nleast that level."];
  C9 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C9\nComponents introduced by\ntwo_of_two_high_sil for frontEnd:\nchannel_b on core 7 at SIL 3."];
  S2 [shape=parallelogram label="S2\nArgument by application of the\ntwo_of_two_high_sil safety architecture\npattern, over each of its general\nscenarios."];
  C10 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C10\nPattern two_of_two_high_sil [koopman, Two\nChannel (2-of-2)]: Two channels, both at\nhigh SIL, compute the same function and\nmust agree. Channel b receives the owner's\nresult, compares it with its own, and\nreturns the verdict to the owner over an\nedge with one initial token; on a\ndisagreement the owner marks its output\ninvalid and the pair goes to its safe\nstate (fails silent), so the fault\nreaction starts within one iteration. No\nexplicit voter: 2-of-2 requires agreement\nrather than a majority, so the comparison\nfolds into channel b and the owner --\nwhich is why this pattern is reachable in\nPhase 4 while NVP is not. The name is\nKoopman's; it is not IEC 61508 MooN\nnotation. Both channels always fire and\nalways produce, per the token-preservation\nobligation. A detected disagreement marks\nthe token invalid; it never withholds it.\nSo the timing guarantee holds in the\ndegraded mode too (Q4), at the price of\nbeing conservative in the fault-free case.\nAttainable SIL [3, 4]; covers random_hw,\nsystematic_sw; fails silent; voter folded."];
  G4 [shape=box label="G4\nA fault in either channel is detected as a\ndisagreement between the two channels.\n[two_of_two_high_sil/SC1]"];
  S3 [shape=parallelogram label="S3\nAchieved through the Comparison tactic\n(Checking): Detection of discrepancies of\nredundant system outputs."];
  C11 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C11\nComparison: Tests whether the outputs of\nfully redundant subsystems are equal in\norder to detect failures. Usually implies\nthe use of a redundancy tactic."];
  C12 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C12\nCandidate methods from the standard: IEC\n61508-7 A.1.3 comparator; IEC 61508-7\nA.6.5 input comparison/voting"];
  G5 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G5\nAn IEC 61508 method realising Comparison\nis implemented in frontEnd, and its\neffectiveness for the faults of concern is\ndemonstrated.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G6 [shape=box label="G6\nA random hardware fault in one channel\ndoes not affect the other channel.\n[two_of_two_high_sil/SC2]"];
  S4 [shape=parallelogram label="S4\nAchieved through the Replication\nRedundancy tactic (Redundancy):\nIntroduction of a redundant system which\nallows detection or masking of random\nhardware failures (not systematic\nfailures)."];
  C13 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C13\nReplication Redundancy: Introduction of a\nredundant system of the same\nimplementation. The redundant systems\nmaintain the same functionality, use\nidentical hardware, and run the same\nsoftware implementation."];
  G7 [shape=box label="G7\nIn the deployed mapping, the replicated\ncomponents run in different fault\ncontainment regions."];
  Sn3 [shape=circle label="Sn3\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nDIFFERENT_FCR between frontEnd and\nfrontEnd/channel_b: cores 1/7, fault\ncontainment regions 1/4, core types\ncortexR/ppcE200."];
  G8 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G8\nCommon-cause failures of these components\nthrough what the placement does not\nseparate (the interconnect and cross-check\nlink, the shared inputs, and the point\nwhere the results are compared or voted)\nare identified and controlled.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  C14 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C14\nCandidate methods from the standard: IEC\n61508-7 A.2.1 tests by redundant hardware;\nIEC 61508-7 A.2.5 monitored redundancy;\nIEC 61508-7 A.3.5 reciprocal comparison by\nsoftware; IEC 61508-7 A.4.5 block\nreplication; IEC 61508-7 A.6.3\nmulti-channel output; IEC 61508-7 A.7.3\ncomplete hardware redundancy; IEC 61508-7\nA.7.5 transmission redundancy"];
  G9 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G9\nAn IEC 61508 method realising Replication\nRedundancy is implemented in frontEnd, and\nits effectiveness for the faults of\nconcern is demonstrated.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G10 [shape=box label="G10\nA systematic fault is not common to both\nchannels. [two_of_two_high_sil/SC3]"];
  S5 [shape=parallelogram label="S5\nAchieved through the Diverse Redundancy\ntactic (Redundancy): Introduction of a\nredundant system which allows detection or\nmasking of failures in the specification\nor implementation as well as random\nhardware failures."];
  C15 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C15\nDiverse Redundancy: Redundant components\nuse different implementations which were\ndeveloped independently from the same\nspecification. Applied at specification\nlevel it further requires the requirement\nspecifications themselves to be produced\nby individual teams."];
  G11 [shape=box label="G11\nIn the deployed mapping, the diverse\ncomponents run on different core types."];
  Sn4 [shape=circle label="Sn4\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nDIVERSE between frontEnd and\nfrontEnd/channel_b: cores 1/7, fault\ncontainment regions 1/4, core types\ncortexR/ppcE200."];
  A4 [shape=ellipse style=filled fillcolor="#fff6d5" label="A4 (A)\nDistinct core types give distinct\ntoolchains and object code, not\nindependent design. Design independence\nremains an undischarged claim. The mapping\nevidence below establishes the deployment\nproperty only."];
  C16 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C16\nCandidate methods from the standard: IEC\n61508-7 A.7.6 information redundancy; IEC\n61508-7 A.13.2 cross-monitoring of\nmultiple actuators; IEC 61508-7 B.1.4\ndiverse hardware; IEC 61508-7 C.4.4\ndiverse programming"];
  G12 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G12\nAn IEC 61508 method realising Diverse\nRedundancy is implemented in frontEnd, and\nits effectiveness for the faults of\nconcern is demonstrated.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G13 [shape=box label="G13\nA detected disagreement does not propagate\nan incorrect result to the consumer.\n[two_of_two_high_sil/SC4]"];
  S6 [shape=parallelogram label="S6\nAchieved through the Voting tactic\n(Masking): Mask the failure of a subsystem\nso that the failure does not propagate to\nother systems."];
  C17 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C17\nVoting: Voting makes a failure\ntransparent. It does not repair the\nfailure, but hides it by choosing a\ncorrect result from redundant subsystems,\ndeciding for the majority of the output\nvalues."];
  C18 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C18\nCandidate methods from the standard: IEC\n61508-7 A.1.4 voter; IEC 61508-7 A.6.5\ninput comparison/voting"];
  G14 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G14\nAn IEC 61508 method realising Voting is\nimplemented in frontEnd, and its\neffectiveness for the faults of concern is\ndemonstrated.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G15 [shape=box label="G15\nActor rasta of application c_rasta,\nrequired SIL 1: the deployment meets the\narchitectural preconditions that the\ncatalog states for SIL 1."];
  C19 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C19\nrasta is unfolded into 1 concurrent copy,\nbound to core 13, implemented at SIL 1."];
  G16 [shape=box label="G16\nEvery copy of rasta is implemented at SIL\n1 or above and runs on a core provisioned\nto at least that level."];
  Sn5 [shape=circle label="Sn5\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nevery active copy of rasta is implemented\nat or above its required SIL and runs on a\ncore provisioned to at least that level."];
  S7 [shape=parallelogram label="S7\nArgument by development process alone: no\nstructural safety pattern is applied."];
  C20 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C20\nIEC 61508 expects diagnostic coverage from\nSIL 2 upward, so process alone is\nadmissible only to SIL 1 (Q20). The\nexploration enforced this ceiling when\nchoosing the pattern."];
  G17 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G17\nrasta is developed and verified to SIL 1\nin accordance with IEC 61508-3.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G18 [shape=box label="G18\nActor powspec of application c_rasta,\nrequired SIL 1: the deployment meets the\narchitectural preconditions that the\ncatalog states for SIL 1."];
  C21 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C21\npowspec is unfolded into 1 concurrent\ncopy, bound to core 13, implemented at SIL\n1."];
  G19 [shape=box label="G19\nEvery copy of powspec is implemented at\nSIL 1 or above and runs on a core\nprovisioned to at least that level."];
  Sn6 [shape=circle label="Sn6\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nevery active copy of powspec is\nimplemented at or above its required SIL\nand runs on a core provisioned to at least\nthat level."];
  S8 [shape=parallelogram label="S8\nArgument by development process alone: no\nstructural safety pattern is applied."];
  C22 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C22\nIEC 61508 expects diagnostic coverage from\nSIL 2 upward, so process alone is\nadmissible only to SIL 1 (Q20). The\nexploration enforced this ceiling when\nchoosing the pattern."];
  G20 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G20\npowspec is developed and verified to SIL 1\nin accordance with IEC 61508-3.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G21 [shape=box label="G21\nActor audspec of application c_rasta,\nrequired SIL 1: the deployment meets the\narchitectural preconditions that the\ncatalog states for SIL 1."];
  C23 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C23\naudspec is unfolded into 1 concurrent\ncopy, bound to core 13, implemented at SIL\n1."];
  G22 [shape=box label="G22\nEvery copy of audspec is implemented at\nSIL 1 or above and runs on a core\nprovisioned to at least that level."];
  Sn7 [shape=circle label="Sn7\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nevery active copy of audspec is\nimplemented at or above its required SIL\nand runs on a core provisioned to at least\nthat level."];
