  G40 [shape=box label="G40\nActor backEnd of application c_rasta,\nrequired SIL 2: the deployment meets the\narchitectural preconditions that the\ncatalog states for SIL 2."];
  C38 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C38\nbackEnd is unfolded into 1 concurrent\ncopy, bound to core 2, implemented at SIL\n2."];
  G41 [shape=box label="G41\nEvery copy of backEnd and of its checker\nis implemented at SIL 2 or above and runs\non a core provisioned to at least that\nlevel."];
  Sn13 [shape=circle label="Sn13\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nevery active copy of backEnd is\nimplemented at or above its required SIL\nand runs on a core provisioned to at least\nthat level."];
  Sn14 [shape=circle label="Sn14\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nevery active copy of backEnd's checker is\nimplemented at or above its required SIL\nand runs on a core provisioned to at least\nthat level."];
  C39 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C39\nComponents introduced by\nlow_sil_diverse_doer_checker for backEnd:\nchecker on core 14 at SIL 2."];
  S16 [shape=parallelogram label="S16\nArgument by application of the\nlow_sil_diverse_doer_checker safety\narchitecture pattern, over each of its\ngeneral scenarios."];
  C40 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C40\nPattern low_sil_diverse_doer_checker\n[koopman, Low SIL Doer/Checker Pair,\narmoush, HtD]: low_sil_doer_checker with\nthe checker additionally required to be a\nDIVERSE implementation on a different\nprocessor type. Added because the encoded\nKoopman catalogue has a hole: at SIL 2 the\nseparated pair defends against random\nhardware faults and the co-located pair\nagainst systematic software faults, but\nnothing covers BOTH. Separation alone does\nnot help against a systematic fault, since\nan identical checker on another core\nreproduces the same mistake. The cost is\nArmoush's heterogeneous-duplex figure: two\nindependent developments, roughly 200% of\none, against ~100% for an identical copy.\nAttainable SIL [1, 2]; covers random_hw,\nsystematic_sw; fails active; voter folded."];
  G42 [shape=box label="G42\nAn implausible output of the doer is\ndetected by the checker.\n[low_sil_diverse_doer_checker/SC1]"];
  S17 [shape=parallelogram label="S17\nAchieved through the Sanity Check tactic\n(Checking): Detection of implausible\nsystem outputs or states."];
  C41 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C41\nSanity Check: Checks whether a system\nstate or value remains within a valid\nrange which can be defined in the system\nspecification or which is based on\nknowledge about the internal structure or\nnature of the system."];
  C42 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C42\nCandidate methods from the standard: IEC\n61508-7 A.1.2 monitoring relay contacts;\nIEC 61508-7 A.2.7 analog signal\nmonitoring; IEC 61508-7 A.3.1-A.3.3\nself-tests; IEC 61508-7 A.4.1-A.4.4\nchecksums; IEC 61508-7 A.5.1-A.5.5 RAM\ntests; IEC 61508-7 A.6.1 test pattern; IEC\n61508-7 A.9 temporal and logical program\nmonitoring; IEC 61508-7 C.3.3 assertion\nprogramming; IEC 61508-7 C.5.3 interface\nchecking"];
  G43 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G43\nAn IEC 61508 method realising Sanity Check\nis implemented in backEnd, and its\neffectiveness for the faults of concern is\ndemonstrated.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G44 [shape=box label="G44\nA random hardware fault in the doer's\nfault containment region does not prevent\ndetection.\n[low_sil_diverse_doer_checker/SC2]"];
  S18 [shape=parallelogram label="S18\nAchieved through the Replication\nRedundancy tactic (Redundancy):\nIntroduction of a redundant system which\nallows detection or masking of random\nhardware failures (not systematic\nfailures)."];
  C43 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C43\nReplication Redundancy: Introduction of a\nredundant system of the same\nimplementation. The redundant systems\nmaintain the same functionality, use\nidentical hardware, and run the same\nsoftware implementation."];
  G45 [shape=box label="G45\nIn the deployed mapping, the replicated\ncomponents run in different fault\ncontainment regions."];
  Sn15 [shape=circle label="Sn15\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nDIFFERENT_FCR between backEnd and\nbackEnd/checker: cores 2/14, fault\ncontainment regions 1/7, core types\ncortexR/cortexM."];
  G46 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G46\nCommon-cause failures of these components\nthrough what the placement does not\nseparate (the interconnect and cross-check\nlink, the shared inputs, and the point\nwhere the results are compared or voted)\nare identified and controlled.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  C44 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C44\nCandidate methods from the standard: IEC\n61508-7 A.2.1 tests by redundant hardware;\nIEC 61508-7 A.2.5 monitored redundancy;\nIEC 61508-7 A.3.5 reciprocal comparison by\nsoftware; IEC 61508-7 A.4.5 block\nreplication; IEC 61508-7 A.6.3\nmulti-channel output; IEC 61508-7 A.7.3\ncomplete hardware redundancy; IEC 61508-7\nA.7.5 transmission redundancy"];
  G47 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G47\nAn IEC 61508 method realising Replication\nRedundancy is implemented in backEnd, and\nits effectiveness for the faults of\nconcern is demonstrated.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G48 [shape=box label="G48\nA systematic fault in the doer's\nimplementation is not reproduced by the\nchecker.\n[low_sil_diverse_doer_checker/SC3]"];
  S19 [shape=parallelogram label="S19\nAchieved through the Diverse Redundancy\ntactic (Redundancy): Introduction of a\nredundant system which allows detection or\nmasking of failures in the specification\nor implementation as well as random\nhardware failures."];
  C45 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C45\nDiverse Redundancy: Redundant components\nuse different implementations which were\ndeveloped independently from the same\nspecification. Applied at specification\nlevel it further requires the requirement\nspecifications themselves to be produced\nby individual teams."];
  G49 [shape=box label="G49\nIn the deployed mapping, the diverse\ncomponents run on different core types."];
  Sn16 [shape=circle label="Sn16\nIndependent re-check of the solution:\nDIVERSE between backEnd and\nbackEnd/checker: cores 2/14, fault\ncontainment regions 1/7, core types\ncortexR/cortexM."];
  A6 [shape=ellipse style=filled fillcolor="#fff6d5" label="A6 (A)\nDistinct core types give distinct\ntoolchains and object code, not\nindependent design. Design independence\nremains an undischarged claim. The mapping\nevidence below establishes the deployment\nproperty only."];
  C46 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C46\nCandidate methods from the standard: IEC\n61508-7 A.7.6 information redundancy; IEC\n61508-7 A.13.2 cross-monitoring of\nmultiple actuators; IEC 61508-7 B.1.4\ndiverse hardware; IEC 61508-7 C.4.4\ndiverse programming"];
  G50 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G50\nAn IEC 61508 method realising Diverse\nRedundancy is implemented in backEnd, and\nits effectiveness for the faults of\nconcern is demonstrated.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  G51 [shape=box label="G51\nNo core hosts software of differing\nintegrity without certified partitioning,\nand every core is provisioned to at least\nthe highest integrity level it hosts."];
  C47 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C47\nKoopman's rule 2: absent certified\npartitioning, all software on a processor\nmust be developed to the highest integrity\nlevel present on it. Whether a core type\ncan provide such partitioning is declared\nper core type by the platform (Q18)."];
  A7 [shape=ellipse style=filled fillcolor="#fff6d5" label="A7 (A)\nSoftware on different cores does not\ninterfere except through the interconnect;\nshared memory, caches and input/output are\nnot modelled, so applying the rule per\ncore assumes freedom from interference\nbetween cores. Certified partitioning,\nwhere used, separates software of\ndifferent SIL on one core in space and\ntime."];
  S20 [shape=parallelogram label="S20\nAchieved through the Barrier tactic\n(Isolation): protect a subsystem from\ninfluences of other subsystems."];
  Sn17 [shape=circle label="Sn17\nIndependent re-check of the solution: all\n28 core slots re-examined against their\ntype ceiling and against the set of\nintegrity levels actually hosted."];
  G52 [shape=box label="G52\nThe deployed mapping and static order meet\nthe declared timing requirements, in the\nfault-free and in the degraded case alike."];
  C48 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C48\nIteration period per application: [1130].\nTiming is a safety property here because a\nreplica that misses its deadline cannot\nperform the check the pattern credits it\nwith."];
  Sn18 [shape=circle label="Sn18\nIndependent re-check of the solution: the\nmapping- and scheduling-aware graph was\nrebuilt from the returned assignment and\nits period computed twice by disjoint\nmethods -- Lawler's parametric\nmaximum-cycle-ratio search and max-plus\nself-timed simulation -- which agree with\neach other, and the period the solver\nreported is the least integer at or above\nthat ratio."];
  Sn19 [shape=circle label="Sn19\nIndependent re-check of the solution: the\nreported period is at least the total\nexecution demand of the busiest core, the\nbound that a static order left open as a\nchain silently loses."];
  G53 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G53\nEvery detected fault brings the function\nto its safe state within the process\nsafety time.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  C49 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C49\nIn the dataflow model, a component's\nverdict on iteration k reaches the owner\nover an edge with t initial tokens, i.e.\nin the owner's firing t iterations later\n(low_sil_diverse_doer_checker: t = 1,\ntwo_of_two_high_sil: t = 1). The reaction\nitself and the process safety time are not\nmodelled."];
  G54 [shape=box style=dashed color="#b00020" label="G54\nEvery software component is developed,\nverified and validated to its required\nsafety integrity level in accordance with\nIEC 61508-3.  <>UNDEVELOPED"];
  C50 [shape=box style="rounded,filled" fillcolor="#f0f0f0" label="C50\nIntegrity levels to be discharged by\nprocess, as active component counts: SIL\n1: 4, SIL 2: 2, SIL 3: 4. Components\nimplemented above their required level\nwere promoted to satisfy Koopman's rule 2;\nthe cost of that promotion is what the\nexploration minimised."];
